  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge shifts eastward towards the southeastern CONUS \nby midweek through the end of the week. West Texas and southeast New \nMexico end up on the western periphery of this ridge. As a result, \ntemperatures gradually tick down after Wednesday (when highs near or \nabove 100 degrees will be common). By the end of the week, highs in \nthe mid-to-upper 90s become more widespread, and temperatures at or \nabove 100 degrees become more confined to the river valleys. \nMeanwhile, deep-layer moisture increases across the area, with PWATs \nnearing the 90th percentile (1.25-1.50 inches). This, coupled with \npassing disturbances in the upper-level flow, leads to increasing \nrain chances for western portions of the area during the latter part \nof the week. As per usual, the best chances will be in/near the \nDavis Mountains (30-60%), with lower chances across the western \nPermian Basin and Upper Trans-Pecos (10-30%). Heavy rain may lead to \nlocalized flash flooding, and some storms may produce strong winds \nduring this timeframe. Eastern portions of the area look to remain \nmostly dry thanks to their relative proximity to the ridge. By the \nweekend, the ridge begins to expand, which results in rain chances \nbecoming more confined to the higher terrain and getting pushed \ntowards our westernmost counties.\n\nSprang",
